{:edb/db-before-t 2740 :edb/db-after-t 2741 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536053 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:51:31.651Z" 13194139536053 true] [17592186133702 1020 #edb/ref 17592186381132 13194139536053 true] [17592186133702 1020 #edb/ref 17592186381159 13194139536053 true] [17592186133740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186381139 13194139536053 true] [17592186133740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186381146 13194139536053 true] [17592186134173 1017 #edb/ref 17592186131480 13194139536053 false] [17592186134173 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381153 13194139536053 true] [17592186134173 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536053 true] [17592186134173 1100 "Find a basis of $H^{⊥}$ if $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$." 13194139536053 false] [17592186134173 1100 "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139536053 true] [17592186134173 1145 "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ \nSo, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381133 13194139536053 true] [17592186381132 1089 "q-140466" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381132 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536053 true] [17592186381132 1100 "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381132 1113 #uuid "de22f8a7-0462-58b6-8ab0-2cf50f198a6b" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381132 1145 "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=15,$ and $c=6.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{15+6}{1}=21.$" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381134 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381135 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381136 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381137 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381138 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1041 "selection" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1104 #edb/ref 17592186381136 13194139536053 true] [17592186381133 1110 #uuid "98349c02-61dc-5524-b5b5-fb5ba1f482a4" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381134 1034 "-9" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536053 true] [17592186381134 1111 #uuid "611c8600-51a3-57ce-89a2-c3c01227cc49" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381135 1034 "9" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536053 true] [17592186381135 1111 #uuid "efab0c1f-6cd1-5135-937d-54493eea3f56" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381136 1034 "21" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536053 true] [17592186381136 1111 #uuid "e0570163-f0f2-562e-845d-70e10361a4fd" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381137 1034 "6" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536053 true] [17592186381137 1111 #uuid "55675eba-ecc0-5f3e-a44c-fcf80b61be4c" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381138 1034 "\\frac{10}{3}" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536053 true] [17592186381138 1111 #uuid "44b5fa61-8321-5c01-88ce-ec336b4dc858" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381139 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381140 13194139536053 true] [17592186381139 1089 "q-140467" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381139 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536053 true] [17592186381139 1100 "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381139 1113 #uuid "6ae892dc-850f-50c8-a715-c60715cd438e" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381139 1145 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =9\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5+4\\cdot 7=36\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =9\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 5+(-4)\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =9\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5+4\\cdot 7=24\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{2}$ only\"." 13194139536053 true] [17592186381140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381141 13194139536053 true] [17592186381140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381142 13194139536053 true] [17592186381140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381143 13194139536053 true] [17592186381140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381144 13194139536053 true] [17592186381140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381145 13194139536053 true] [17592186381140 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 131941395