 [17592186381146 1145 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$ \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 7=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-14)=0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 7=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-3)\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-14)=0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 7=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-14)=0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  \"${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$\"" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381148 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381149 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381150 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381151 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381152 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1041 "selection" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1104 #edb/ref 17592186381151 13194139536053 true] [17592186381147 1110 #uuid "bfb37e99-2a82-5daa-a730-6aa4ed3b9a5f" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381148 1034 "${v}_{3}$ only" 13194139536053 true] [17592186381148 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536053 true] [17592186381148 1111 #uuid "188710dd-bf64-5655-a7b7-23f736ead657" 131941395360